    "tier": "contested",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "Session gravity is the engine: the October 2, 1963 date pulls Lee Morgan (fresh off The Sidewinder) and Andrew Hill (just before his avant-garde breakout on Point of Departure) into a hard-bop context with Philly Joe Jones, while the March 7 date brings Donald Byrd and Herbie Hancock \u2014 two elite Blue Note front lines under one leader. AllMusic gives 4 stars (Jurek: 'a very gritty and realist approach to the roots of hard bop. Highly recommended'), Penguin Guide 3.5 stars, and the title track and 'Three Way Split' are Mobley originals with staying power in the working hard-bop repertoire.",
    "case_against": "The consensus is thinner than the dossier implies: Penguin 3.5 is below the core-collection threshold (4 stars + Crown), and the dossier's other three 'sources' (Wikipedia, iTunes, MusicBrainz, Cover Art Archive) are metadata, not critical consensus \u2014 leaving an effective critical-source count of two, at the bottom edge of min_sources: 3. No NPR, DownBeat critics'-poll, or JazzTimes canon-list citation appears. It is not Mobley's defining statement \u2014 Soul Station (1960) and Workout (1961) are the peaks \u2014 and the two-session split (entirely different front lines) means it is not a single coherent musical statement but two strong dates packaged together.",
    "disagreement": "The references split on tier: one reads the consensus as thin and the album as secondary in Mobley's catalogue (contested), the other reads the session gravity as overwhelming and the two-session split as minor (consensus_core). The real divide is whether the Hill-Morgan session alone is enough to overcome the thin sourcing and the lack of a unified album concept.",
    "recommendation": "Include if John wants the Mobley arc filled out past its Soul Station/Workout peak; the October Hill-Morgan date is the draw, but the consensus sourcing does not meet the consensus_core bar and the two-session package weakens the defining-statement case.",
